---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PubItem from '../components/PubItem.astro';
import PromptMatrix from '../components/viz/PromptMatrix.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { byTheme } from '../data/publications';

const meaning = byTheme('meaning');
const talks = meaning.filter((p) => p.type === 'talk');
const chapters = meaning.filter((p) => p.type === 'chapter');
---
<Base title="AI, philosophy & religion" description="Talks, book chapters and essays by Furkan Özçelik on artificial intelligence, large language models, philosophy of mind and religion.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Ideas</p>
      <h1>AI, philosophy <em>&amp;</em> religion</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Drawn to the old questions that new machines make urgent again: what minds are, what understanding is, and what
        it means when a system trained on human text starts to reason about God, value and meaning.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="llm-h">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker"><span class="numeral">i</span>Talks</p>
        <h2 id="llm-h">Arguments for God, <em>asked</em> of a language model</h2>
        <p class="body">
          Large language models are increasingly consulted on questions of belief. Two talks — at the AI, Philosophy and
          Religion conference in Warsaw (May 2024) and at Religion, Logic and AI in Sinaia (September 2023) — explored how
          ChatGPT and other LLMs assess classical arguments for God’s existence, and how those assessments change with the
          prompt and with the identity the model is asked to adopt.
        </p>
        <div>{talks.map((p) => <PubItem p={p} />)}</div>
      </div>
      <div class="split__fig"><PromptMatrix /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="chapters-h">
    <div class="wrap split split--text">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker"><span class="numeral">ii</span>Book chapters</p>
        <h2 id="chapters-h">Writing for a <em>Turkish</em> readership</h2>
        <p class="body">
          Two chapters in edited Turkish volumes introduce artificial intelligence, deep learning and large language models
          to readers in the humanities, and discuss the philosophical and religious implications of these technologies — along
          with the dangers and debates surrounding AI.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>{chapters.map((p) => <PubItem p={p} />)}</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="found-h">
    <div class="wrap split split--text">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker"><span class="numeral">iii</span>Community</p>
        <h2 id="found-h">Science Philosophy Religion <em>Foundation</em></h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="body">
          Co-founder of the Science Philosophy Religion Foundation in Turkey, dedicated to dialogue between science,
          philosophy and religion. Wide reading across the three ends up, in part, in the notebook as essays and book notes.
        </p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/writing/">Read the notebook <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr); gap: clamp(28px, 6vw, 80px); align-items: start; }
  .split--text { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr); }
  .split h2 { font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.2vw, 2.5rem); }
  .split .body { color: var(--text-2); }
  .split__fig { position: sticky; top: calc(var(--header-h) + 24px); }
  @media (max-width: 860px) { .split, .split--text { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .split__fig { position: static; } }
</style>
